Our design recognizes eyes from iris texture, following the Ma et al., 2003 paper.
We isolate and standardize the iris, describe its texture with numerical features, 
and compare these with known eyes. Below, we explain the paper's choices and our 
implementation changes.

Step 1: Separate training and testing (IrisRecognition.py)

Following the paper's session-based split (Ma et al., 2003 4.3), we train on 
session 1 and test on session 2 to evaluate recognition of the same eyes under 
different photo conditions. Our dataset has 108 eyes, with 324 training and 432 test 
images. We use the supplied still images directly, as opposed to the quality selection 
conducted in the paper (Ma et al., 2003 3.1).

Step 2: Locate the iris (IrisLocalization.py - Ma et al. 2003, 3.2.1)

Following the paper, we approximate the pupil boundary and the iris-sclera boundary
with two circles and use the texture between them. We estimate the pupil position
from darkest rows/column and group connected dark pixels within a 120x120 window,
treating the largest group as the pupil.

We simplify the paper’s method by estimating the pupil circle directly from the 
center and area of the largest connected dark region, without further edge-based refinement. 
We detect the outer iris circle using OpenCV’s Hough transform, restricting its radius to 
80–130 pixels and selecting the circle centered nearest the pupil. If none is detected, we 
use the pupil center and a radius of 105 pixels.

Step 3: Normalize the iris (IrisNormalization.py - Ma et al. 2003, 3.2.2)

Following the paper, we unwrap the iris region into a 64x512 rectangular image so that 
images with different iris sizes and deformations can be compared more consistently. 
We sample between the pupil and outer iris boundaries, allowing for the pupil being 
off-center within the iris.

Step 4: Image Enhancement (ImageEnhancement.py - Ma et al. 2003, 3.2.3)

Following the paper, we estimate illumination from 16x16 block means, enlarge and
subtract that estimate, then equalize contrast within 32x32 blocks. This reduces
uneven lighting and brings out local texture. We additionally rescale the corrected
image to 0-255 for OpenCV's histogram equalization.

Step 5: Describe the texture (FeatureExtraction.py - Ma et al. 2003, 3.3)

Following the paper, we keep the 48 rows nearest the pupil, where it reports useful
texture with less eyelid/eyelash interference. Its two spatial filters capture
texture at different scales, emphasizing horizontal variation while retaining
information from other directions. For each 8x8 block, we record mean response
magnitude and average absolute deviation to describe texture strength and
variation. This produces 1,536 features per image.

We retain the paper's filter widths with chosen frequencies of 1/8 and 1/16.

Step 6: Learn and match known eyes (IrisMatching.py - Ma et al. 2003, 3.4)

Following the paper, we use Fisher linear discriminant analysis (LDA) to reduce 
the 1,536 texture features while emphasizing differences between eyes and reducing 
variation within the same eye. We learn this projection using only training images, 
then apply it to both training and test features.

We use the paper’s seven training rotations, from -9 to 9 degrees in 3-degree steps, 
to accommodate small eye rotations caused by head tilt or positioning. For each eye, 
we average its training features separately at each rotation to create seven reference centers. 
We compare a test image with these centers and keep the smallest distance for each eye, 
then predict the closest eye. As in the paper, we evaluate L1, squared L2, and cosine distance.

Our dataset contains 108 eye classes, so LDA permits at most 107 dimensions. 
We use this limit instead of the paper’s 200 dimensions.

Step 7: Evaluate recognition (PerformanceEvaluation.py - Ma et al. 2003, 4.3)

Following the paper, we evaluate identification using correct recognition rate
(the percentage assigned the correct eye ID) and verification using cosine
distance thresholds. Verification measures incorrect claims accepted (false match
rate) and correct claims rejected (false nonmatch rate). Varying the threshold
shows the tradeoff between these errors.

We compare L1, squared L2, and cosine distance using all 1,536 original features and 107 LDA dimensions. 
We also evaluate cosine recognition rates at 20, 40, 60, 80, 100, and 107 dimensions to examine how 
dimensionality reduction affects accuracy. For verification, we use cosine distance with 107 dimensions 
and select acceptance thresholds that keep the observed false match rate within 0.1%, 1%, and 5%. 
We report the corresponding false nonmatch rates on the same test images. 
Our tables and plots report single measured values without the paper’s bootstrap confidence intervals.

Limitations and improvements:

Our simplified boundary detection can mistake shadows or eyelashes for the pupil,
and its fixed radius limits and fallback circle may locate the iris incorrectly.
Checking boundary quality and refining uncertain detections could reduce errors
that carry into normalization and feature extraction.

We process every image without screening for blur or masking eyelids and eyelashes.
Selecting clearer images and excluding covered regions from texture measurements
could make matching more reliable.

Evaluation covers only 108 eyes, and verification thresholds are selected and
measured on the same test images. Testing fixed thresholds on additional images
from more eyes and capture conditions would better assess performance beyond
this dataset.
